          {
            "content": [
              {
                "text": "In recent years, infrared small target detection technologies\nhave become increasingly significant in multiple fields, includ\ning ecological environment monitoring[1], marine rescue[2],\n[3] and military surveillance[4], [5]. This is because infrared\nlight exhibits a longer working range and stronger penetration\ncapability compared to visible light. In IRSTD tasks, targets\nare generally very small, weak, and are often embedded\nin complex backgrounds. In IRSTD tasks, targets typically\ninclude drones, aerostats, and ships, which commonly exhibit\nthe following characteristics[6]: (i) Low target energy: Due to\nsevere signal attenuation over long-distance propagation, the\nreceived infrared signal is weak. This results in low contrast\nbetween the target and strong background clutter, making the\ntarget highly susceptible to being obscured by background\nnoise. (ii) Small target size: At long imaging ranges, targets\noccupy only a few pixels in the image and lack discernible\ntexture or structural details. (iii) Complex background: The\noperational environment often contains numerous interfering\nobjects that introduce edges, corners, and other salient features,\nsignificantly complicating the accurate discrimination of small\ntargets from the background. Some examples are shown in\nFig. 1. Therefore, efficiently and accurately detecting infrared\nsmall targets remains a critical challenge.",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "n1",
            "type": "paragraph"
