  "frontMatter": {
    "abstract": [
      {
        "text": "Abstract—The success of deep learning in infrared small\ntarget detection (IRSTD) relies on large-scale, precise pixel-level\nannotations, yet their high acquisition cost and inevitable noise\nimpede further progress. Recently, point-supervised learning has\ndemonstrated remarkable potential in mitigating these issues\nin IRSTD. The dominant paradigm involves generating pixel\nlevel pseudo-labels  point annotations, then applied to train\ndeep segmentation networks. However, some proposed pseudo\nlabel generation strategies rely on measuring intensity similarity\nand spatial proximity between pixels to classify them. This\napproach leads to an unreasonable target probability distribution\nwherein, even among pixels that truly belong to the target,\nthose close to the certain point, typically annotated point, are\nassigned high probabilities, while those farther away receive\nsignificantly lower probabilities. To address this, we propose\na shape-prior-driven framework—Gradient-Guided Minimumfrom\nVariance Growth Filter with Deep Learning Refinement—that\nproduces high-quality pseudo-labels by modeling edge intensity\nand closure, followed by iterative refinement with a deep seg\nmentation network. Leveraging structural consistency over pixel\nlevel similarity, our approach effectively handles irregular and\nlow-saliency targets. Crucially, our method avoids distance-based\nassumptions, yielding robustness to point annotation deviations.\nThe proposed method generally outperforms existing approaches\nas validated by plenty of experiments on three representative\ndatasets.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
